WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:03.030 align:start position:0%
 
my story begins  with my mom's sister and i being  evacuated by u.s marines from war-torn  lebanon  to cyprus i remember finding a cotton  candy machine  on the battleship we were on and just  for a fleeting moment  i could enjoy being a kid not a kid  terrified of what he's just experienced  not a kid terrified of what lies ahead  after five long days we finally arrived  in the seattle airport  and a strong smell of coffee welcomed us  to our new home  and our new way of life now this might  seem like a triumphant moment  that now we're saved but it's not always  that simple  as we made our way home i remember a mix  of emotion  we were joyful and relieved but at the  same time nervous  are we going to be okay here  with sudden change comes sudden maturity  and even at a  young age of 11 years old i realized i  had opportunities here in the united  states i didn't have  back in lebanon i was absolutely  fascinated by  the brain and mathematics and i dreamt  that one day  i want to go to college and i want to  become a neuroscientist  just like my hero oliver sacks  as we settled into life in seattle  everything seemed bright  but then one day our foundation  came crashing down my dad had his second  heart attack  but this time it was different this time  he lost his job medical bills piled up  we actually had to relocate from our  home to government housing to afford  rent  i felt as if the ground beneath us began  to crumble  and i started asking myself am i going  to be okay  will i still be able to attend college  one day  i'm still going to become a  neuroscientist right  as the years passed by my dream of  attending college  became more like a figment of my  imagination  i started noticing a growing gap between  myself and my friends  while they during their summers went to  summer camps for robotics coding science  and sports  i stayed home while they received new  computers and video game consoles  for their birthdays i did not  while they started touring college  campuses with their families and getting  private tutors to help them with  sats and personal statements my family  could barely afford a car to get around  with  at this time i was nearing the end of my  high school years  so i had to face my reality and make a  decision  is college still a possibility for me or  should i  pursue something more practical and get  a job and support my family financially  and i had discussions with my family  about college and it was  full of uncertainty questions like even  if i get into college who's going to pay  for it  what are these scholarships and are they  a scam  why neuroscience maybe i could pick  something easier  what if this whole college thing this  whole  university this whole academia science  thing is just not made for families  like us a pivotal moment came  when one of our family friends laura who  had known me since i was uh two years  old  and knew how bad i wanted to go to  college basically forced me to apply  for a high school summer research  internship at the university of  washington  i was so sure that i wouldn't get into  the internship  why pick me out of all the kids but to  my  surprise i got chosen the  the feeling that somebody out there  actually saw potential in me  even though at that time i didn't see  potential in myself  is what inspired me to actually go to  the internship  now on the the end of the first day of  my internship  i thought i had made a huge huge huge  mistake  the kids around me they were so smart so  confident  they already knew how to code it's it's  as if they've trained for this moment  their whole life  i i just didn't belong there i should go  back  on the second day of the internship i  learned i was placed in a lab that dealt  with  a left ventricular assist device which  is a system that you implant for heart  failure patients  and in that moment i really thought  about my dad and his heart problems  and that intrigued me what is this  device and how could i ever contribute  to this device into these people  and that's what pushed me to go in the  third day  on the third day i was given my first  task which is to code up  a small piece of code to simulate the  heat properties of one of  the components that have to go inside  the patient and  we have to make sure that it doesn't  burn the tissue of the patient  i'm terrified i've never coded before  i've never even opened a  program before why did they pick me why  not just another student who should do  it  but at that moment i had to stop and  think  what if i don't take this opportunity  what would my life look like  what if i'm forever constrained by the  financial  circumstances that were handed down to  me  but in that moment i decided to take  control  and it took me two weeks to create this  little piece of code  but in those two weeks i created that  piece of code and little did i know  that this little piece of code would  create the  coil design for the component that  selected out of many in the lab  would end up being used for the first  clinical trials the system underwent  and not only that but it turned out  later on that my coil design was as  close to optimal  as it could be and this is the first  time that i felt the ground  solidify and that the first step i took  in this path  wasn't muddy that it didn't sink me and  i didn't make a fool of myself  that actually this is a path that i can  take and that i want to take  i could go back to my family and say  that i have a contribution and impact to  make in the world and that i can do it  and that college might just be the thing  for me  inspired i uh got into  applied and got into the university of  washington  took part of ever more computational  pieces of work in research at anything  from  seizure predictions to models of visual  cortex  and i'm proud to say that here at mit  i've actually  had the privilege of teaching  programming and how to code to incoming  graduate students in the department and  now as part of the jazieri lab  i studied the computational basis of  counter factual thinking  the same sort of cognitive processes  that went on in that pivotal  moment when i thought what if i take  this opportunity  but what if i don't and today i share my  story  for those of you out there who feel like  me and i want to tell you  that you don't have to be constrained by  the financial circumstances you were  born in  that you can follow the passions of your  heart and the most important thing you  have is not money or connections  but the belief people have in you and  the belief that you have in yourself  and i'm here to say to you that i  believe in you  thank you

